# Federal Manpower Portal — project flow

## How we work
- Build **one step at a time**. Finish a step, summarize what changed, wait for OK before the next.
- Keep code **simple and readable**. No hidden magic, no unused fields “for later”.
- Prefer clear names over clever abstractions. User must be able to change anything easily.
- Scope is flexible: drop or simplify features when the user says so. Update this rule if domain terms change.
- **No AI-flavored comments** — no “This component handles…”, “Fetch the data”, “TODO: implement”, or narrating obvious code. Comment only when a non-obvious business rule needs a short note. Prefer clear names so comments are rarely needed.

## Stack
- Laravel 12 + **Livewire 4** + Alpine
- UI: `public/css/portal.css` + Google Fonts / Alpine / Livewire CDN assets — **no Vite, no `npm run dev`, no rebuild on change** (same style as DIG)
- Do not add `@vite` to layouts. Edit CSS in `public/css/portal.css` directly.
- Patterns: follow `D:\laravel\DIG_Software_management` (Livewire panels, Support classes, custom permissions)
- Private client files: `storage/app/private/...` only — never public disk

## Domain (plain language)
| Term | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| Client | Job-seeker / applicant (not employer) |
| Company | Overseas employer **or** university (`employer` / `university`) |
| Care Off | Person/company who referred or submitted the case (optional) |
| Process status | Stage master (Applied, Visa applied, etc.) |
| Overall status | `active` / `inactive` on each client |
| Direct vs referred | No Care Off vs linked Care Off |

## Do not add unless asked
- Care Off margin / commission / payout fields
- Invite-only client register (register is **public**)
- Extra packages or layers without a clear need

## Structure preference
- Business logic in small `app/Support/*` classes when shared
- One Livewire component per screen/feature
- Config-driven nav/permissions (`config/portal.php`) when that step is built
- Migrations and models with obvious column names
- Blade/PHP/JS: clean markup and code; no decorative comment banners or emoji in source
